{:kind :reply, :text "\n\n(define conv:source (behavior:source))\n(define conv:user-scan (behavior:scan conv:source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:history (behavior:latest-value conv:user-scan (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:reply (network [content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id \"assistant-reply\" :time 0 :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> (event:point \"assistant-reply\" 0 ack) conv:source)\n    ack)))\n(define conv:system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"To reply, call (conv:reply \\\"your message here\\\") as executable Lain source. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation replies.\"))\n(define conv:user-msgs (behavior:map conv:user-scan (network [prefix] (seq:project :content prefix))))\n(define conv:user-latest (behavior:latest-value conv:user-msgs (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:user-content (seq:last conv:user-latest))\n(define conv:user-id (data:get :id user-ingress))\n(define conv:user-time (data:get :time user-ingress))\n(define conv:assistant-time (+ conv:user-time 1))\n(define conv:assistant-id (text:format \"assistant-%s\" conv:user-time))\n(define conv:reply-fn (network [content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id conv:assistant-id :time conv:assistant-time :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> (event:point conv:assistant-id conv:assistant-time ack) conv:source)\n    ack)))\n(define conv:all-msgs (behavior:map conv:user-scan (network [prefix]\n  (seq:concat\n    (seq:append (seq:empty) conv:system-msg)\n    (seq:map (network [rec] (data:record :role (data:get :role rec) :content (data:get :content rec))) prefix)))))\n(define conv:latest-msgs (behavior:latest-value conv:all-msgs (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:request (model:request (data:record :id (text:format \"req-%s\" conv:user-time) :messages conv:latest-msgs)))", :thinking "Let me analyze the task carefully:\n\n1. I need to build a live conversation network in Lain\n2. Key requirements:\n   - Capture user-ingress occurrences with event:point (it's a message RECORD, not a behavior)\n   - Build conversation history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan with seq:append)\n   - Connect user-ingress to model requests\n   - Define a reusable reply function that delivers to user AND adds acknowledged reply to history\n   - Subsequent requests contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n   - System message with reply-function calling convention\n   - Don't send request until user event arrives\n   - Use different name for conversation accumulator (not \"history\" which is the parent binding)\n   - model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as connection target\n   - Assistant time = user time + 1, assistant id must differ from user id\n   - message-to-user acknowledges a record with :id :time :role \"assistant\" :content\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n1. **User ingress capture**: user-ingress is a message record with :id, :time, :role \"user\", :content. I need to capture its occurrences with event:point. Since user-ingress is a cell that publishes records, I can use event:point with data:get to extract fields reactively.\n\n2. **Conversation history accumulator**: Use behavior:scan with seq:append to accumulate message records. I'll call it `conv:history` (not \"history\" which is the parent binding).\n\n3. **User message to history**: When a user message arrives, add it to conv:history.\n\n4. **Model request**: When a user message arrives, build a request with system message + conversation history, then call model:request.\n\n5. **Reply function**: A callable that takes content, delivers via message-to-user, and adds the acknowledged reply to conv:history.\n\n6. **Assistant reply to history**: After model responds, the reply function adds the acknowledged record to history.\n\nWait, let me re-read the constraints:\n\n- \"Connect user-ingress to model requests\" - when user message arrives, trigger a model request\n- \"define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history\"\n- \"Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\"\n- \"Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling your reply function with a new assistant message\"\n- \"Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention\"\n- \"Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history\"\n- \"Use the same model throughout\"\n- \"The parent binding history retains your generated source code automatically; it is not conversation history. Use a different name for the conversation accumulator.\"\n- \"memory-cell is a reference-valued plain record. Read its fields with data:get.\"\n- \"Do not send a request until a user event arrives.\"\n- \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nLet me think about the flow:\n\n1. user-ingress publishes a record like {:id \"user-1\" :time 1 :role \"user\" :content \"hello\"}\n2. I capture this with event:point into a behavior:source\n3. I scan it with seq:append to build a vector of user messages\n4. When a user message arrives, I need to:\n   a. Add it to conversation history\n   b. Build a model request with system + history + new user message\n   c. Call model:request\n\n5. The model responds with executable Lain that calls my reply function\n6. The reply function:\n   a. Calls message-to-user to deliver\n   b. Adds the acknowledged reply to conversation history\n\nWait, but the model's response IS the reply. The model generates Lain source that calls the reply function. So the flow is:\n\n- User message arrives → build request → model generates source → source is admitted → source calls reply function → reply function delivers to user and adds to history\n\nLet me think about how to connect user-ingress to model requests reactively.\n\nThe user-ingress is a cell. When it publishes a record, I can use event:point to capture it as a temporal occurrence. Then I can scan to build history.\n\nFor triggering the model request: I need to react to new user messages. Since model:request must be invoked (not connected), I need to call it. But in a propagator network, how do I trigger a call when a new value arrives?\n\nActually, looking at the dynamic-expressions-cell: \"A function call whose operands are cells is reactive: it recomputes when their strongest values change.\"\n\nSo if I write `(model:request (build-request conv:history-latest))` and conv:history-latest changes, the request is re-invoked. But I need to be careful - I don't want to re-send old requests.\n\nActually, let me think differently. The user-ingress publishes records. I can:\n1. Capture user messages into a behavior source via event:point\n2. Scan to build a vector of user message records\n3. Project the latest user message\n4. Build the conversation messages vector (system + history + latest user)\n5. Call model:request reactively\n\nBut the issue is: model:request should only fire when there's a NEW user message, not on every recomputation. However, in a propagator network, if the input changes, the call re-fires. Since user-ingress only publishes new records (each is a new occurrence), the scan will produce new prefixes, and the latest-value will change, triggering a new request.\n\nWait, but I also need to add assistant replies to history. The reply function adds to a separate source. So I need TWO sources feeding into the conversation history:\n- User messages source\n- Assistant replies source\n\nAnd I need to merge them chronologically. Actually, let me use a single source for the conversation history, and both user messages and assistant replies feed into it.\n\nLet me design:\n\n```\nconv:user-source (behavior:source) - receives user message occurrences\nconv:assistant-source (behavior:source) - receives assistant reply occurrences\nconv:all-source (behavior:source) - receives ALL conversation occurrences (both user and assistant)\nconv:history-scan (behavior:scan conv:all-source seq:append (seq:empty))\nconv:history-latest (behavior:latest-value conv:history-scan (seq:empty))\n```\n\nWait, but I need to feed both user and assistant into the same source. Let me use a single source:\n\n```\nconv:source (behavior:source)\n```\n\nUser messages feed into it via event:point from user-ingress.\nAssistant replies feed into it via event:point from the reply function's message-to-user output.\n\nThen scan with seq:append to build the history vector.\n\nFor the reply function:\n```\n(define conv:reply (network [content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id \"assistant-reply\" :time 0 :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> (event:point (data:get :id ack) (data:get :time ack) ack) conv:source)\n    ack)))\n```\n\nWait, but the reply function is called from model-generated source. The model generates something like `(conv:reply \"Hello! How can I help?\")`. This needs to work in the live network.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The reply function needs to:\n1. Deliver via message-to-user\n2. Add the acknowledged reply to history\n\nBut message-to-user returns the record with :acknowledged true. I need to feed that into the conversation source.\n\nThe issue with event:point inside a network callable: \"Callback invocation uses a frozen temporary network, so callbacks must use their explicit arguments and immutable constants; reading changing external cells does not read the live network.\"\n\nHmm, but a network callable isn't a callback in the behavior:scan/map sense. Let me re-read: \"A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation; model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback.\"\n\nSo the reply function should NOT be inside a scan/map. It should be called directly in the live network. The model generates source like `(conv:reply \"Hello\")` which is admitted and runs in the live network.\n\nLet me think about the reply function more carefully. It needs to:\n1. Call message-to-user with an assistant record\n2. Feed the acknowledged result into conv:source as a temporal occurrence\n\nBut event:point needs an id and time. The assistant id must differ from user id, and time = user time + 1.\n\nActually, I think the reply function can be simpler. The model generates the content, and the reply function handles delivery and history. But the id and time need to be unique.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The model's response is Lain source. It will be something like:\n```\n(conv:reply \"Hello! I'm here to help.\")\n```\n\nThe reply function:\n```\n(define conv:reply (network [content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id \"assistant\" :time 0 :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> (event:point \"assistant-reply\" 0 ack) conv:source)\n    ack)))\n```\n\nBut wait - the id and time need to be unique per reply. If I use constants", :finish-reason "stop", :truncated? false}